   "author": "Hollowmere team (wilderness round 5)",
   "story": "The Ember Creek hot springs come up through the rock at the head of a remote valley, and for sixty years the Vail family ran a bathhouse over them: a plunge hall round a tiled pool, a steam room fed off the spring, the boiler and pump room in the cellar, four guest cabins along a boardwalk and a rock pool out in the open air. When the Bloom came down the valley the Cordon made the springs a holding site. Guests and travellers off the road were ordered to stay, the front doors were nailed shut from outside and a soldier was left on the desk. Connie Vail kept the water hot because she believed the heat would cook the sickness out; Emil kept the boiler fed for her and Walt kept the cabins. The warm, wet dark was the best thing that ever happened to the Bloom: it took the steam room first and glows there still. Connie locked herself in the owner's suite with the strongbox and the spare key went down to Emil in the cellar. Nobody came back for the guests.",
